The authoritative list is nv_attach_impl.cpp:436-534.

Listeners (gum_interceptor_attach through register_hook):
- __cudaRegisterFatBinary (dlsym RTLD_NEXT), __cudaRegisterFunction, __cudaRegisterVar, __cudaRegisterFatBinaryEnd;
- cudaMalloc, cudaMallocManaged;
- cudaMemcpyToSymbol, cudaMemcpyToSymbolAsync.

Replacements (gum_interceptor_replace through replace_hook_once):
- cudaLaunchKernel, cudaLaunchKernel_ptsz, cuLaunchKernel;
- cuGraphAddKernelNode(_v2), cuGraphExecKernelNodeSetParams(_v2), cuGraphKernelNodeSetParams(_v2);
- cudaMemcpyFromSymbol, cudaMemcpyFromSymbolAsync;
- cuLibraryLoadData.

In substitution mode:
- The four registration listeners return at once (nv_attach_impl_frida_setup.cpp:264-269).
- cudaMemcpyFromSymbol(Async) pass straight to the original unless the late path is active (frida_setup.cpp:1064-1092).

Agent-level interpositions: the agent also interposes __cudaRegisterFatBinary, to register the LLVM VM factory, then calls the original (agent.cpp:841-857; exported in agent.version), and wraps main through __libc_start_main and bpftime_hooked_main (agent.cpp:561-582). bpftime_hooked_main only runs bpftime_agent_main before main and returns main's value.

Not hooked (unfiltered grep over attach/nv_attach_impl, runtime/agent and runtime/src/attach):
- cudaDeviceSynchronize, cudaStreamSynchronize, cuCtxSynchronize, cudaEventSynchronize;
- plain cudaMemcpy and cuMemcpy*;
- cudaGraphLaunch and cuGraphLaunch;
- cudaDeviceReset and exit.
The cuCtxSynchronize at nv_attach_impl.cpp:1364 is bpftimetool's run-on-cuda path, and the cudaDeviceSynchronize at default_trampoline.cu:733 is the trampoline's host test code. cuLaunchHostFunc, cuStreamAddCallback and stream memory operations are never used.

Documentation/bpf/standardization/instruction-set.rst:492-551 defines BPF_ATOMIC for 32- and 64-bit operands:
- imm BPF_ADD, BPF_OR, BPF_AND or BPF_XOR;
- the optional BPF_FETCH modifier, which returns the old value in src;
- BPF_XCHG;
- BPF_CMPXCHG, which compares with R0 and loads the old value, zero-extended, into R0.

Atomic means the operation cannot be interrupted or corrupted by other access to the same memory region by other BPF programs or means outside the specification (495-497). The 6.8 docs state no memory ordering: a grep for memory model, ordering or barrier in Documentation/bpf found nothing.

The arm64 JIT (arch/arm64/net/bpf_jit_comp.c:490-526) emits:
- STADD, STCLR, STSET or STEOR for the non-fetch forms, with no acquire/release (relaxed by the arm64 definition, inferred);
- LDADDAL, LDCLRAL, LDSETAL or LDEORAL for the fetch forms, SWPAL for xchg and CASAL for cmpxchg (acquire+release).

clang emits the non-fetch form for __atomic_add_fetch with an unused result (cuda-counter object, insn 28: lock *(u64 *)(r0 + 0x0) += r6) and for __sync_fetch_and_add with an unused result (threadscheduling object, insns 59 and 82).

On the GPU, llvmbpf lowers every BPF atomic RMW to an LLVM atomicrmw with Monotonic ordering and the default sync scope (vm/llvm-jit src/compiler_utils.cpp:372-401, compiler.cpp:1637-1689). Saved PTX of a probe from the 11-09 feasibility run shows an unscoped atom.add.u64 after the lookup (results gpu/feasibility-20261109/gpu-env/attach-probe/probe_raw.ptx:74, .target sm_61, PTX 5.0). That file's generator is not established, so it is inferred that dev's llvmbpf emits the same unscoped atom.

BPF_CMPXCHG in llvmbpf (compiler.cpp:1690-1718, read, not run): R0 is set from a plain load of the target taken before the cmpxchg (beforeVal), and the cmpxchg's own result is discarded, so R0 is not atomic with the exchange. When another thread writes in between, a CAS loop can see R0 == expected although the exchange failed (a lost update), or R0 != expected although it succeeded (applied twice) (inferred). BPF_XCHG uses atomicrmw xchg with fetch (compiler.cpp:1682-1689). The patch overlay vm/compat/llvm-vm/*.patch has no atomic changes (grep).

There is one request slot per process (CommSharedMem, gpu_helper_protocol.h:69-84). host_call (default_trampoline.cu:129-174) loops turn = 0..31. In each turn only the lane with laneid == turn:
1. takes the per-module GPU lock and the per-process occupy_flag;
2. writes the request while holding both;
3. issues membar.sys and sets flag1;
4. spins on flag2;
5. reads the response and releases both locks.
Then __syncwarp(active) runs. Within one helper call the lanes of a warp are therefore served one at a time in ascending lane order, and no lane returns until all 32 turns are done. Warps are served in the order they win the GPU lock, with no fairness. The watcher serves one request at a time (bpf_attach_ctx_cuda.cpp:303-330).

A lookup answers with the device address of the host value (bpf_attach_ctx_cuda.cpp:133-144; gpu_address_of_map_value 559-574): device and managed memory as is, host memory through cuMemHostGetDevicePointer of the registered segment. The GPU's later loads, stores and atomics through that pointer go straight to host memory without another host call. Update, delete, printk and puts answer an int.

Recent changes and map-type routing:
- Since 1b3d9c99, ordinary ARRAY lookups and updates use the device address directly (default_trampoline.cu:265-275, 336-353).
- Since aa6eb973, pid_tgid comes from a module global (:410-422).
- 1501 has no device path: lookup and update fall through to host_call.
- On the Orin, 1501 is created as an ordinary HASH (bpftime_shm.hpp:137-154; syscall_context.cpp:567-576).

Sites: 3 bpftime host-call sites, eliminate, each in its own program of benchmark/gpu/micro/cuda_probe.bpf.c:
- :163: update(hash_map, key 0, 1, BPF_ANY);
- :171: lookup, then non-atomic (*val)++;
- :182: delete key 0.

The loader enables one mode and reads no map. In lookup and delete modes nothing inserts key 0, so by the code the lookup always returns NULL and the delete returns ENOENT (survey reasons).

Lockstep today: 32 identical serialized updates (idempotent, final value 1), 32 NULL lookups, 32 ENOENT deletes.

Status: PR #624 "feat: add conservative GPU SIMT verification" is OPEN (author yunwei37, created 2026-08-01, updated 2026-11-12). Head 7aca4027 is local ref official/codex/gpu-simt-verifier-clean; 22 files, +3086/-20. It shares the PREVAIL pin fd85b8a6 with dev. Its files bpftime-verifier/src/gpu/{uniformity_analysis,simt_safety_check,gpu_platform}.{hpp,cpp} are byte-identical on official/revision/automatic-warp-execution. That branch's gpu_verifier.cpp differs only by phase timing (diff of the two copies) and adds warp_execution_eligibility.{hpp,cpp}.

Where it runs: in the agent, the target CUDA process, inside nv_attach_impl::create_attach_with_ebpf_callback when the CUDA link is instantiated, before any PTX patching. See the PR #624 diff hunk in attach/nv_attach_impl/nv_attach_impl.cpp (new lines 226-256) and official/revision/automatic-warp-execution:attach/nv_attach_impl/nv_attach_impl.cpp:251-335. Inputs:
- data.instructions (the stored program, prog->get_insns());
- map descriptors built from the fd-indexed MapBasicInfo vector;
- section name = program name;
- verifier_mode from runtime config (PR diff in runtime/src/attach/bpf_attach_ctx.cpp).
It is compiled only with ENABLE_EBPF_VERIFIER (PR diff in attach/nv_attach_impl/CMakeLists.txt). STRICT returns GPU_VERIFIER_REJECTED (-4096), which aborts init_attach_ctx_from_handlers.

Pipeline (PR head gpu_verifier.cpp:267-306):
(1) Reject unknown 15xx map types.
(2) Run PREVAIL on a shadow program: `mov r0, 8` before calls with out-params, out-params modelled as writable memory (51-186, 224-260). It uses gpu_platform_spec: sections must contain "cuda__" or "__memcapture" (gpu_platform.cpp:193-204, 255-259); maps 1501→HASH, 1502/1503/1504/1512/1513→ARRAY, 1527→RINGBUF (261-277); a fixed helper table for 1, 2, 3, 6, 14, 25 and 501-511 (108-173). Any other helper throws (288-295).
(3) analyze_uniformity.
(4) check_simt_safety.

Uniformity analysis (uniformity_analysis.hpp:18-72; .cpp): a forward worklist over raw eBPF instructions, not PREVAIL's CFG (760-819; successors 306-349). The result is UniformityAnalysisResult{success, error_message, states[pc] (input state at pc), reachable[pc]}. UniformityState holds:
- regs[11] of Uniformity {UNKNOWN, UNIFORM, VARYING};
- pointers[11] of PointerProvenance {region NONE/UNKNOWN/STACK/MAP_VALUE, constant_offset, offset_uniformity, pointee_uniformity, may_target_shared_map};
- map_fds[11];
- stack_bytes[512];
- stack_pointer_slots.
The join is element-wise: UNKNOWN is bottom, and UNIFORM joined with VARYING gives VARYING (267-304, 691-703).

Sources of uniformity:
- The helper return table: VARYING for 2, 3, 6, 25, 505 (thread_idx), 509 (sm_id) and 511 (lane_id); UNIFORM for 14, 501, 502, 503 (block_idx), 504, 506, 507, 508 and 510 (warp_id).
- Out-params of 503/504/505/508 are written to stack bytes with the helper's uniformity (646-670).
- A lookup's result takes the uniformity of its key bytes (418-429). The returned pointer is MAP_VALUE, and VARYING if the map may be per-thread (1502/1512 or an unknown fd) (394-416, 628-644).
- Loads from a MAP_VALUE pointer take pointee_uniformity (595-606); other non-stack loads are VARYING (609).
- Atomic stores mark their stack bytes VARYING; fetch and cmpxchg results are VARYING (534-554).
- LDDW src=1 records the map fd (444-451); mov copies it (461-465); ALU and LDX reset it (468-490, 561).

SIMT check (simt_safety_check.cpp:174-295) reports a list of SimtSafetyError{pc, check_name, message} for:
- lane-varying branch predicates (204-215);
- non-uniform atomic addresses (217-221);
- lane-varying stores into possibly-shared map values (223-229);
- prohibited helpers 501 and 506 (250-255);
- non-uniform keys of lookup/update/delete (257-266);
- non-uniform update flags or values (268-280);
- non-uniform printk arguments (282-291).

Output: only std::optional<std::string> escapes verify_gpu_program (gpu_verifier.hpp:12-15). Per-pc states are internal.

On the branch, evaluate_warp_execution_eligibility re-runs the pipeline. It rejects any atomic (warp_execution_eligibility.cpp:132-134); helpers 25, 6, 501 and 507 (163-173); stores through UNKNOWN or per-thread pointers (141-156); update/delete on maps that are not shared GPU maps 1501/1503/1504/1513, so ordinary HASH/ARRAY host-call maps make a program ineligible (176-197); and non-uniform update values (198-207). The result is one boolean per program (221-251). It is stored in nv_attach_entry.warp_auto_execution (nv_attach_impl.cpp:316-334, 349), passed to the pass as RuntimeInput.warp_auto_execution (pass/ptxpass_core/include/ptxpass/core.hpp:76-93), and lowered to a leader-lane prefix at the hook site (core.cpp:403-470: %laneid, activemask, vote.ballot.sync). It is gated by env BPFTIME_GPU_AUTO_WARP_EXECUTION (nv_attach_impl.cpp:189-204).

Cost (upstream, x86): uniformity 2.2-2.5 ms and SIMT 0.01-0.03 ms against PREVAIL 0.58-1.9 s for 4,096 instructions (device-verifier-phase-attribution-20260904.md on the branch).

Uniform vs varying: only PR #624's uniformity analysis has lane-variance semantics. The per-site query is query_pointer_uniformity(states[pc], 2, key_size) (uniformity_analysis.cpp:711-741), with sources from the helper table (gpu_platform.cpp:110-171). It is cheap: about 2 ms for 4,096 instructions in upstream's x86 timing. It is compiled into bpftime-verifier with the PREVAIL headers and built only with ENABLE_EBPF_VERIFIER (PR diff in bpftime-verifier/CMakeLists.txt). analyze_uniformity itself does not call PREVAIL (read).

Its constraints:
- no constant lattice;
- no control dependence; sound only together with the branch check (simt_safety_check.cpp:204-215);
- aborts on local calls or unknown helpers (gpu_platform.cpp:288-295);
- loses map fds through spills (561).
LLVM's own analyses lack helper-aware sources and LLVM 15 has no UniformityAnalysis (inferred, see 3c).

Literal constant: nothing ready.
- dev's reachability pass does register constant propagation but does not model the stack: ST is ignored, and LDX yields a value only from frozen constant maps (bpftime_prog_reachability.cpp:183-216). It cannot see `key = 0` stored to r10-4.
- PREVAIL's stack array domain should hold such a literal (inferred), but it is reachable only through to_set() strings.
- In LLVM the literal is a store into the escaped stack slot before the call (observed PTX `st.local.u32 [%rd1+508], 0`). It needs MemorySSA-style lookup (inferred).

Survey context: of the 26 host-call sites the survey labelled key_scope "constant" (the schema does not define the term; callsite-survey scripts/workflow.js:18):
- 21 use &pid = helper 14 >> 32, a per-process run-time value: example/gpu/cuda-counter, rocm-counter, cudagraph and gpu_shared_map:55;
- 3 use a literal 0 (benchmark/gpu/micro/cuda_probe.bpf.c:161, 170, 181);
- 2 are printk without a key.
Since aa6eb973, helper 14 reads a module global the agent writes (default_trampoline.cu:410-422; nv_attach_impl.cpp:1355). The pid is known in the agent at PTX generation time.

Concrete bytecode (cuda-counter cuda__probe, local .bpf.o):
- pc 22-23: r2 = r10-4 (key slot holding pid);
- pc 24: r1 = map call_count (LDDW);
- pc 26: call 1;
- pc 27: `if r0 == 0 goto +2`;
- pc 28: `lock *(u64*)(r0+0) += r6` (r6 = 1);
- pc 29: goto out;
- pc 30-37: r2 = r10-4, r3 = r10-48 (holding 1), r1 = call_count, r4 = 1 (BPF_NOEXIST), call 2.
Variants in the same tree:
- non-atomic: retprobe pc 31-41, lookup, null check, ldx/add/stx, then update(r3 = r0, r4 = 2 BPF_EXIST);
- read-modify-write via update(BPF_ANY): cuda_probe.bpf.c:211-219, also seen in PTX.

What must hold, and who can tell:
- Same map fd at both calls: PR #624 map_fds[1] per pc, as long as the fd is not spilled; PREVAIL r1.map_fd; LLVM ConstantInt arg0.
- Same key slot with no store in between: PR #624 STACK provenance with constant_offset, plus a scan for intervening stores; LLVM gep offset plus a MemorySSA clobber check (inferred).
- The null edge leads to the NOEXIST update and the non-null edge to an atomic ADD at offset 0 whose only use is the add: a CFG pattern plus use information (see 4a).
- The update value equals the increment: stack contents. PR #624 does not track values; PREVAIL stack cells would (inferred).

Fit: a bytecode-level pattern matcher over PR #624-style per-pc state, extended with value constants and a use list, at the point where MapBasicInfo is available (the agent, create_attach_with_ebpf_callback). Or an LLVM pattern in generate_ptx with MemorySSA, if map info is added to the request (inferred). PREVAIL is a poor fit: no lookup-pointer identity, no fetch atomics, and an order of magnitude more cost. Nothing in the tree recognises the idiom today.

verdicts.detail:
evaluate_warp_execution_eligibility first requires the full verify_gpu_program to pass: PREVAIL plus uniformity plus SIMT (warp_execution_eligibility.cpp:233-239). It then rejects at the first reachable:
- atomic (132-134);
- plain ST/STX through an UNKNOWN pointer, or into a MAP_VALUE that may be per-thread (141-156);
- helper 25, 6, 501 or 507 (163-173);
- update/delete whose map is unresolved, unknown, per-thread (1502/1512), or not in {1501, 1503, 1504, 1513} (176-197);
- update with non-uniform value bytes (198-207).

The SIMT check (simt_safety_check.cpp:204-291) additionally rejects:
- lane-varying branches;
- non-uniform atomic addresses;
- lane-varying stores to shared map values;
- PROHIBITED helpers 501 and 506;
- non-uniform keys for lookup/update/delete;
- non-uniform update flags or values;
- non-uniform printk payloads.

Helper table (gpu_platform.cpp:108-171):
- UNIFORM: 1 (but lookup follows key uniformity, uniformity_analysis.cpp:418-429), 14, 501, 502, 503, 504, 506, 507, 508, 510.
- VARYING: 2, 3, 6, 25, 505, 509, 511.
- PROHIBITED: 501, 506.

may_target_shared_map is true for every map type except 1502/1512 (uniformity_analysis.cpp:406-416). So the store rule admits plain stores into CPU HASH/ARRAY values reached by a lookup, even though update/delete on CPU maps is rejected.

Both the kprobe_entry and the kretprobe pass honor warp_auto_execution (kretprobe main.cpp:38-50 on that branch). memcapture does not.

On dev's Orin, 1501 would arrive as map_type 1 (HASH) through map_basic_info (bpftime_shm.hpp:142-154; bpf_attach_ctx_cuda.cpp:542-546). A port of these rules would then treat 1501 updates as non-shared (inferred).

The CUDA hooks are only those in nv_attach_impl.cpp:379-534:
- Listeners: __cudaRegisterFatBinary/Function/Var/FatBinaryEnd, cudaMalloc, cudaMallocManaged, cudaMemcpyToSymbol(Async).
- Replacements: cudaLaunchKernel, cudaLaunchKernel_ptsz, cuLaunchKernel, cuGraphAddKernelNode(_v2), cuGraphExecKernelNodeSetParams(_v2), cuGraphKernelNodeSetParams(_v2), cudaMemcpyFromSymbol(Async), cuLibraryLoadData.
- The agent additionally exports __cudaRegisterFatBinary (agent.version).

An unfiltered `git grep` for gum_interceptor_attach/replace finds only those two helper lambdas (nv_attach_impl.cpp:397, 423) plus the generic Frida uprobe code.

Not hooked:
- any *Synchronize: the only references are bpftimetool's cuCtxSynchronize at nv_attach_impl.cpp:1364, the trampoline test main, and map/unit-test code;
- plain cudaMemcpy/cuMemcpy*;
- cudaGraphLaunch/cuGraphLaunch;
- cuLaunchKernelEx/cudaLaunchKernelExC or cooperative launches (grep finds none);
- exit.
No cuLaunchHostFunc or cuStreamAddCallback is used.

contradictions:
Which calls are host calls. The task premise lists CPU maps, 1501, printk 6 and puts 501 (and formerly ARRAY and pid). device-side shows a wider set, confirmed:
- deletes on every GPU array type 1502/1503/1504/1512/1513 (only ARRAY with extra_buffer short-circuits; default_trampoline.cu:368-384);
- every 1527 lookup/update/delete (no device branch, so they fall to host_call; 233-384);
- helper 14 when the module global is 0 (410-422);
- ordinary ARRAY whenever gpu_address_of_array_values returns NULL: counting builds, a missing binding mutex, a failed pin, or an unreachable address (bpf_attach_ctx_cuda.cpp:480-503).

gaps_filled.fact:
Per-site information can ride in an unused helper argument instead of a distinct callee. The trampoline signatures leave free:
- lookup 0001(map,key,a,b,c): r3-r5;
- update 0002(map,key,value,flags,a): r5;
- delete 0003: r3-r5.
printk 0006 and perf_event_output 0025 use all five arguments. In the 11-10 PTX sample, unused arguments are uninitialized registers, so a value would have to be set explicitly (in IR: a call-operand change).

map_info is written into a module only when the module loads:
- write_trampoline_globals, called from load_library_with_probes (nv_attach_impl.cpp:2366-2385, 2457);
- the late path, fatbin_record (nv_attach_fatbin_record.cpp:263-292);
- directly-run (nv_attach_impl.cpp:1323-1352).
Substituted images cannot be unloaded, and 'a later session's probes cannot be patched into it'. A decision or table baked into a substituted module therefore lives as long as the module, and map_info there is never refreshed. A rerun of late bootstrap does replace late-path module sets (nv_attach_impl.cpp:249-262).

Probe function names carry no program identity:
- '__probe_func__'+kernel (kprobe_entry main.cpp:52-55);
- '__retprobe_func__'+kernel (kretprobe);
- '__memcapture__'+count.
Passes chain on one module. Inferred: two kprobe-entry programs on the same kernel would emit two definitions of the same .func, and generated per-site callees need names unique per program and module.
